          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for an uncomplicated urinary tract infection in a non-pregnant adult?",
          "answer": "**Emergency note first:** If you have fever with back or side pain, shaking chills, vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down, confusion, or symptoms that get worse, seek care promptly. Fever, a fast heartbeat, low blood pressure, and confusion together can signal sepsis, which is an emergency. Call emergency services.\n\n**What the guidance covers.** An uncomplicated urinary tract infection (UTI) is acute bladder infection (cystitis) in a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function. Pregnancy, men, catheters, kidney stones, structural problems, and weakened immunity are all managed on a different pathway (infection-and-urology guidance, \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection\").\n\n**Diagnosis and testing.** With typical symptoms and no vaginal discharge or irritation, that guidance says empiric treatment without a urine culture is appropriate. A urine dipstick adds little here: a positive result confirms what was already likely, and a negative result does not reliably rule infection out. Culture is generally reserved for uncertain diagnoses, complicated or recurrent infection, or treatment failure (general clinical reference, \"Urinary tract infection\").\n\n**Antibiotic choice and duration.** The passages I retrieved do not name first-line antibiotics or treatment lengths, so I cannot tell you what guidance recommends on those points. Please check the full guideline or ask a clinician.\n\n**Where authorities differ.** For mild cystitis, some European guidance describes offering symptom relief with delayed or no antibiotics as a shared decision, because some episodes resolve without them. Other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default, citing symptom duration and the risk of kidney involvement. The disagreement is about how much antibiotic exposure to avoid, not about the underlying facts.\n\n**Recurrence.** Recurrent UTI is conventionally defined as two or more episodes in six months, or three or more in a year. Described prevention options include behavioral changes, vaginal estrogen after menopause, and, for frequent and disabling recurrence, antibiotic prevention weighed against resistance risk. Cranberry products and extra fluids have limited and mixed evidence.\n\n**Evidence strength.** The retrieved passages do not grade the strength of evidence behind these recommendations, so I can't say how firm each one is.\n\nA clinician can confirm the right approach for your situation, including whether testing or a different pathway applies.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
